<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Nieuwe Republiek</h1><p class="st-meta">1884 CE – 1888 CE · capital Vryheid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Boer republic carved out of Zululand in 1884 as payment for helping Dinuzulu win the Zulu throne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zululand</a> fell into civil war. In 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucas-meyer">Lucas Meyer</a>, among them the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a>, backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinuzulu-kacetshwayo">Dinuzulu</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zibhebhu-kamaphitha">Zibhebhu</a>, and in return claimed a huge tract of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> land, where they proclaimed the Nieuwe Republiek with Lucas Meyer as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksraad-dutch-east-indies">Volksraad</a> and founded its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vryheid">Vryheid</a>. Its original claim covered much of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zululand</a>, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> forced a reduction in 1886, recognising a smaller territory in exchange for the rest being left to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep farmers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Nieuwe Republiek joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic</a> in 1888 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vryheid">Vryheid</a> district. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> the district was transferred to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/natal-colony">Natal Colony</a> in 1903, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucas-meyer">Lucas Meyer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> became leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> generals.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nieuwe-republiek.jpg" alt="Nieuwe Republiek" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nieuwe_Republiek.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1884 CE – 1888 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vryheid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lucas Meyer</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1886&amp;focus=nieuwe-republiek" aria-label="Nieuwe Republiek on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5488 3195 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nieuwe Republiek lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5808 3451L5822 3471M5808 3451L5746 3380"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5822 3471h0M5746 3380h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5808 3451h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5808 3451h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vryheid. <a href="/atlas?year=1886&amp;focus=nieuwe-republiek">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
